## Features and usage

The test program takes in three arguments:
1. input_size: number of integers (supplied tester requires at least 2).
2. cutoff: the cutoff level where thread-spliting stops and serial sorting is used.
3. seed: controls the randomly generated numbers.

Format:
./test-mergesort <input_size> <cutoff> <seed>

Example:
./test-mergesort 100000 4 6767

How it works:
The program creates two threads. Each thread will sort the right and left halves of the array. When both threads finish their results are merged.
If there isn't enough memory to prepare the threads, the whole section is sorted without creating the threads. If a thread cannot be started, the current thread will sort that half.
The code that creates an argument struct is responsible for freeing it when it is no longer needed. The tester frees the first argument. Each parent thread frees its children’s arguments after they finish, or immediately if they cannot be started.

## Testing

### Custom correctness tests

The custom tests tested 14 different inputs with 3 cutoff levels: 0, 1 and 3. This gives a total of 42 test cases where all passed.

The inputs inside the test include:
- Already sorted and reverse-sorted values.
- Equal values and all zeros.
- Duplicates and mixed positive and negative values.
- Negative-only and already sorted negative values.
- INT_MIN and INT_MAX, including repeated extremes.
- Single-element and two-element arrays.
- Even-sized and odd-sized arrays.
- A descending array of 100 elements.

The testing works by coppying the input into two array. One is sorted using qsort() and the other using our implementation. The two arrays are compared to measure accuracy. The results are then recorded in an CSV.
The custom correctnest test program can be run from the project directory:

clang -Wall -std=gnu89 -pthread testing/test.c mergesort.c -o testing/test

./testing/test
Running the testing program will write the results into testing/correctness.csv

### Supplied tester
Using the supplied tester 60 cases passed using sizes 2, 5, 11, 100, and 10001 with cutoffs of 0, 1, 2, 3, 5, and 6 with seeds of 1234 and 6767.
The results can be seen in testing/supplied-results.csv

Using the supplied tester, four runs with 100 million elements were tested and completed successfully.

### Performance

For performance, 100 million elements using seed 1234 at cutoff levels 0, 2, 4 and 6 were tested. With each cutoff level tested four times, This gave us 16 runs. 
The results are recorded in testng/performance.csv

| Cutoff | Trial 1 | Trial 2 | Trial 3 | Trial 4 | Average time | Speedup |
| 0      | 16.10 s | 16.07 s | 16.58 s | 16.40 s | 16.2875 s    | 1.00x   |
| 2      | 4.52 s  | 4.61 s  | 4.67 s  | 4.56 s  | 4.5900 s     | 3.55x   |
| 4      | 2.38 s  | 2.42 s  | 2.52 s  | 2.42 s  | 2.4350 s     | 6.69x   |
| 6      | 2.53 s  | 2.50 s  | 2.58 s  | 2.41 s  | 2.5050 s     | 6.50x   |

The speedup is calculated by dividing average serial time by average parallel time. From the results, cutoff 4 had the fastest speedup. While increasing the cutoff level to 6 did improve performance.
Additional thread overhead and hardware limits can reduce the benefit of creating more threads.
